  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6adf36233c68','A2'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Look at the flag. It shows the cross of St George. Which country''s flag is it?',array['England','Scotland','Wales','Ireland']::text[],'A','The red cross of St George on white is the flag of England.',array['life_in_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/flag_england.png','A flag','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:ecbfc54ab1bf','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When is St David''s Day?',array['17 March','23 April','1 March','30 November']::text[],'C','''St David''s Day, for Wales, is on 1 March.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Each country of the UK has a patron saint with a special day. St David''s Day, for Wales, is on 1 March. St Patrick''s Day, for Northern Ireland, is on 17 March. St George''s Day, for England, is on 23 April, and St Andrew''s Day, for Scotland, is on 30 November. Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their patron saint''s day as an official holiday.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:1527bad0e508','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who is the patron saint of Scotland?',array['St Andrew','St George','St David','St Patrick']::text[],'A','''St Andrew''s Day, for Scotland''.',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Each country of the UK has a patron saint with a special day. St David''s Day, for Wales, is on 1 March. St Patrick''s Day, for Northern Ireland, is on 17 March. St George''s Day, for England, is on 23 April, and St Andrew''s Day, for Scotland, is on 30 November. Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their patron saint''s day as an official holiday.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:4720cdf1489d','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Patron saints: which statement is true?',array['St Patrick''s Day is an official holiday in Northern Ireland.','St George''s Day is an official holiday in England.','St David''s Day is in April.','Scotland has no patron saint.']::text[],'A','Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their saint''s day as an official holiday.',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Each country of the UK has a patron saint with a special day. St David''s Day, for Wales, is on 1 March. St Patrick''s Day, for Northern Ireland, is on 17 March. St George''s Day, for England, is on 23 April, and St Andrew''s Day, for Scotland, is on 30 November. Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their patron saint''s day as an official holiday.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:cb4d1ae496b4','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When was the Magna Carta agreed?',array['in 1666','in 1215','in 1588','in 1066']::text[],'B','''In 1215, King John was forced...''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','In 1215, King John was forced by his noblemen to agree to a number of their demands. The result was a charter of rights called the Magna Carta, which means ''Great Charter''. It established the idea that even the king was subject to the law. It protected the rights of the nobility and restricted the king''s power to collect taxes or to make or change laws.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:ef13e2ab18f4','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What important idea did the Magna Carta establish?',array['that even the king had to obey the law','that Parliament must have two houses','that the king could make any law','that everyone must pay more taxes']::text[],'A','''Even the king was subject to the law.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','In 1215, King John was forced by his noblemen to agree to a number of their demands. The result was a charter of rights called the Magna Carta, which means ''Great Charter''. It established the idea that even the king was subject to the law. It protected the rights of the nobility and restricted the king''s power to collect taxes or to make or change laws.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:67cffcd10763','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who forced King John to agree to the Magna Carta?',array['the Romans','the French king','his noblemen','the farmers']::text[],'C','''Forced by his noblemen''.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','In 1215, King John was forced by his noblemen to agree to a number of their demands. The result was a charter of rights called the Magna Carta, which means ''Great Charter''. It established the idea that even the king was subject to the law. It protected the rights of the nobility and restricted the king''s power to collect taxes or to make or change laws.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:f62378956707','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did the NHS begin?',array['in 1948','in 1945','in 1966','in 1918']::text[],'A','''The NHS began in 1948.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The National Health Service (NHS) began in 1948. Its aim was to give everyone in the UK healthcare that is free at the point of use. The minister responsible for setting it up was Aneurin (Nye) Bevan. To see a family doctor, called a GP, you usually need to register at a local surgery first.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:0a8776b5a6c2','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which minister set up the NHS?',array['Florence Nightingale','Winston Churchill','Alexander Fleming','Aneurin Bevan']::text[],'D','''The minister responsible... was Aneurin (Nye) Bevan.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The National Health Service (NHS) began in 1948. Its aim was to give everyone in the UK healthcare that is free at the point of use. The minister responsible for setting it up was Aneurin (Nye) Bevan. To see a family doctor, called a GP, you usually need to register at a local surgery first.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:8154a98cda7c','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do you usually need to do before you can see a GP?',array['register at a local surgery','buy medicine','go to a hospital first','call 999']::text[],'A','''You usually need to register at a local surgery first.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The National Health Service (NHS) began in 1948. Its aim was to give everyone in the UK healthcare that is free at the point of use. The minister responsible for setting it up was Aneurin (Nye) Bevan. To see a family doctor, called a GP, you usually need to register at a local surgery first.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:2b0a8f5a7ff7','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do people wear for Remembrance Day?',array['white roses','green ribbons','blue badges','red poppies']::text[],'D','''People wear red poppies.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Remembrance Day, on 11 November, commemorates those who died fighting for the UK and its allies. It began after the First World War, which ended at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918. People wear red poppies, and there is a two-minute silence at 11 a.m.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:1584bf412a06','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How long is the silence at 11 a.m. on Remembrance Day?',array['five minutes','one minute','two minutes','eleven minutes']::text[],'C','''There is a two-minute silence at 11 a.m.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Remembrance Day, on 11 November, commemorates those who died fighting for the UK and its allies. It began after the First World War, which ended at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918. People wear red poppies, and there is a two-minute silence at 11 a.m.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:47a4f38681dd','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which war ended on 11 November 1918?',array['the First World War','the Second World War','the Battle of Hastings','the Crimean War']::text[],'A','''The First World War, which ended at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Remembrance Day, on 11 November, commemorates those who died fighting for the UK and its allies. It began after the First World War, which ended at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918. People wear red poppies, and there is a two-minute silence at 11 a.m.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:fc6e64cb29ee','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the minimum age to vote in UK general elections?',array['18','16','21','25']::text[],'A','''For UK general elections you must be at least 18.''',array['life_in_uk','dialogue']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Officer: To vote, you need to be on the electoral register.
James: How old do I need to be?
Officer: For UK general elections you must be at least 18.
James: And how often are general elections held?
Officer: At least every five years.',null,null,null,'practice'),
