  ('ai:ai-batch-03:65225f1ff482','B1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','What is the capital city of Northern Ireland?',array['Dublin','Belfast','Londonderry','Cardiff']::text[],'B','Belfast is the capital of Northern Ireland; Dublin is the capital of Ireland.',array['life_in_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:e28cc61512c9','B1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Hadrian''s Wall, in the north of England, was built by the ___.',array['Romans','Normans','Vikings','Saxons']::text[],'A','The Roman emperor Hadrian ordered the wall to be built.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:275e178addd1','B1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','In 1066, William of Normandy defeated King Harold at the Battle of ___.',array['Bosworth','Hastings','Trafalgar','Waterloo']::text[],'B','The Norman Conquest began with the Battle of Hastings in 1066.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:9cf1427e124b','B1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Which famous writer was born in Stratford-upon-Avon?',array['Robert Burns','William Shakespeare','Charles Dickens','Jane Austen']::text[],'B','Shakespeare was born in Stratford-upon-Avon in 1564.',array['life_in_uk','culture']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6b949334e430','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the Great Fire of London in 1666, the new St Paul''s Cathedral was designed by Sir Christopher ___.',array['Drake','Brunel','Wren','Wordsworth']::text[],'C','Sir Christopher Wren designed the new St Paul''s Cathedral after the Great Fire of London.',array['britain','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:0491e65d49c2','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','St Augustine became the first Archbishop of ___.',array['Westminster','London','York','Canterbury']::text[],'D','St Augustine, a missionary from Rome, became the first Archbishop of Canterbury.',array['britain','history','culture']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:f34c15e60bde','B1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Look at the Union Flag. It combines the crosses of three patron saints. Which country''s saint is NOT shown?',array['Ireland','Wales','England','Scotland']::text[],'B','The Union Flag has the crosses of St George, St Andrew and St Patrick; Wales is not represented.',array['life_in_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/union_flag.png','The Union Flag','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:52a0106a23d0','B1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Look at the Union Flag. The red diagonal cross stands for which patron saint?',array['St Andrew','St David','St George','St Patrick']::text[],'D','The Union Flag combines the crosses of St George (England), St Andrew (Scotland) and St Patrick (Ireland); St Patrick''s cross is the red diagonal one.',array['britain','picture']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/union_flag.png','The Union Flag','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:f58b4be281a5','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Whose daughter was Elizabeth I?',array['Charles I''s','Henry VII''s','Henry VIII''s','James I''s']::text[],'C','''Elizabeth I, the daughter of Henry VIII''.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Elizabeth I, the daughter of Henry VIII, became queen in 1558. She was popular with her people, partly because of the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588, a large fleet of ships sent by Spain to invade England. Her reign also saw a flowering of English drama, with playwrights such as William Shakespeare.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:24e064a5d885','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the Spanish Armada?',array['a royal wedding','a fleet of ships sent to invade England','a trade agreement','a Spanish army that captured London']::text[],'B','''A large fleet of ships sent by Spain to invade England.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Elizabeth I, the daughter of Henry VIII, became queen in 1558. She was popular with her people, partly because of the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588, a large fleet of ships sent by Spain to invade England. Her reign also saw a flowering of English drama, with playwrights such as William Shakespeare.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d4ef2dae45cb','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Elizabeth I: which statement is supported by the text?',array['She was defeated by Spain.','English drama flourished during her reign.','She became queen in 1588.','She was unpopular with her people.']::text[],'B','''Her reign also saw a flowering of English drama.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Elizabeth I, the daughter of Henry VIII, became queen in 1558. She was popular with her people, partly because of the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588, a large fleet of ships sent by Spain to invade England. Her reign also saw a flowering of English drama, with playwrights such as William Shakespeare.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:398aa299c107','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did the Great Fire of London start?',array['on a ship','in a bakery','in Parliament','in a church']::text[],'B','''A fire broke out in a bakery in Pudding Lane.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','In 1666 a fire broke out in a bakery in Pudding Lane and spread across much of the City of London, destroying thousands of houses and many churches, including St Paul''s Cathedral. The architect Sir Christopher Wren was responsible for rebuilding St Paul''s, which still stands today.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:26c9300ec017','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who rebuilt St Paul''s Cathedral?',array['Isaac Newton','Sir Christopher Wren','Isambard Kingdom Brunel','Henry VIII']::text[],'B','''Sir Christopher Wren was responsible for rebuilding St Paul''s.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','In 1666 a fire broke out in a bakery in Pudding Lane and spread across much of the City of London, destroying thousands of houses and many churches, including St Paul''s Cathedral. The architect Sir Christopher Wren was responsible for rebuilding St Paul''s, which still stands today.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d915c1dda190','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were members of the Women''s Social and Political Union called?',array['Cavaliers','Chartists','Roundheads','suffragettes']::text[],'D','''Its members became known as suffragettes.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, women campaigned for the right to vote. In 1903 Emmeline Pankhurst founded the Women''s Social and Political Union, whose members became known as suffragettes. In 1918 women over the age of 30 were given the vote, and in 1928 women were given the vote at 21, the same age as men.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d6494e5b226f','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did women get the vote on the same terms as men?',array['1945','1928','1903','1918']::text[],'B','''In 1928 women were given the vote at 21, the same age as men.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, women campaigned for the right to vote. In 1903 Emmeline Pankhurst founded the Women''s Social and Political Union, whose members became known as suffragettes. In 1918 women over the age of 30 were given the vote, and in 1928 women were given the vote at 21, the same age as men.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d8a568ba07ec','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which mountain does the passage describe as the highest in England?',array['Ben Nevis','Scafell Pike','Windermere','Snowdon']::text[],'B','''Scafell Pike, at 978 metres, is the highest mountain in England.''',array['britain','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Lake District in north-west England is the country''s largest national park. Its long, narrow lakes and steep valleys were carved by glaciers during the last Ice Age. Windermere is the biggest stretch of water, and Scafell Pike, at 978 metres, is the highest mountain in England. Sheep farming has shaped the landscape for centuries, and the area inspired the poet William Wordsworth.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d506840fc261','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How were the Lake District''s long, narrow lakes formed?',array['by dams built on the rivers','by sheep farmers digging them','by glaciers during the last Ice Age','by volcanic eruptions']::text[],'C','''Its long, narrow lakes and steep valleys were carved by glaciers during the last Ice Age.''',array['britain','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Lake District in north-west England is the country''s largest national park. Its long, narrow lakes and steep valleys were carved by glaciers during the last Ice Age. Windermere is the biggest stretch of water, and Scafell Pike, at 978 metres, is the highest mountain in England. Sheep farming has shaped the landscape for centuries, and the area inspired the poet William Wordsworth.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:3e36e1db3c25','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which poet was inspired by the Lake District, according to the passage?',array['Robert Burns','Dylan Thomas','William Wordsworth','William Shakespeare']::text[],'C','''The area inspired the poet William Wordsworth.''',array['britain','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Lake District in north-west England is the country''s largest national park. Its long, narrow lakes and steep valleys were carved by glaciers during the last Ice Age. Windermere is the biggest stretch of water, and Scafell Pike, at 978 metres, is the highest mountain in England. Sheep farming has shaped the landscape for centuries, and the area inspired the poet William Wordsworth.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:ca4839db3fd3','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why does Amira want the job?',array['to move to Manchester','to earn more money at weekends','to use languages to help refugees','to learn Spanish at work']::text[],'C','''I''ve read about your work with refugees in Manchester, and I''d like to use my languages to help.''',array['dialogue','britain']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Interviewer: Why do you want to work for us?
