  ('ai:ai-batch-03:da9df4637650','B2'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Florence Nightingale is famous for her work as a ___ during the Crimean War.',array['engineer','nurse','journalist','soldier']::text[],'B','She is often seen as the founder of modern nursing.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:72d8b02f9818','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Skara Brae on Orkney is the best-preserved prehistoric ___ in northern Europe.',array['castle','bridge','village','cathedral']::text[],'C','Skara Brae, off the north coast of Scotland, is the best-preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe.',array['britain','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6cfd66b3f235','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Geoffrey Chaucer''s collection of stories told by pilgrims on their way to Canterbury is called ___.',array['The Lord of the Rings','The Canterbury Tales','Pride and Prejudice','Paradise Lost']::text[],'B','Chaucer wrote The Canterbury Tales, stories told by pilgrims travelling to Canterbury; it was one of the first books printed by William Caxton.',array['britain','culture','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:fa228924f96d','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Look at the timeline. How many years passed between the Norman Conquest and the Magna Carta?',array['151','249','149','139']::text[],'C','1215 - 1066 = 149.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/uk_timeline.png','Five dates in British history','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:b6bd66936a35','B2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Watch the timeline. Which event came between the Magna Carta and the Great Fire of London?',array['the Norman Conquest','the Act of Union','the Battle of Waterloo','the Spanish Armada']::text[],'D','1215 Magna Carta, 1588 Spanish Armada, 1666 Great Fire.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'video','samples/uk_timeline.mp4','Five dates in British history (10 seconds)','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:5371c3a7c62d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the passage, what did Churchill''s speeches do?',array['They ended the war.','They won the 1945 election.','They created the NHS.','They raised public morale.']::text[],'D','His speeches ''helped to raise public morale''.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:70e90259be00','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What happened to Churchill in 1945?',array['He retired from politics for good.','He lost the general election.','He surrendered to Germany.','He became Prime Minister for the first time.']::text[],'B','''He was defeated in the general election of 1945.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:bd3871bc0f1d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Churchill: which statement is true according to the text?',array['He never lost an election.','He became Prime Minister after the war ended.','He supported surrender in 1940.','He served as Prime Minister on two separate occasions.']::text[],'D','1940 and again from 1951.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d3874be62254','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which fuel powered the steam machines?',array['electricity','wood','coal','oil']::text[],'C','''Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel''.',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6f570c52b074','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What social change does the passage describe?',array['people leaving cities for farms','people moving from the countryside to cities','fewer factories being built','shorter working hours for children']::text[],'B','''Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities.''',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:7e53c37c6380','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the passage on the Industrial Revolution, ''harsh'' is closest in meaning to ___.',array['unusual','severe','temporary','pleasant']::text[],'B','Harsh conditions are severe and unpleasant.',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:1f2ce3de1d3a','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who began building the Tower of London, according to the passage?',array['Queen Victoria','Alfred the Great','Henry VIII','William the Conqueror']::text[],'D','''The Tower of London was begun by William the Conqueror soon after his victory at Hastings in 1066.''',array['britain','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Standing on the north bank of the River Thames, the Tower of London was begun by William the Conqueror soon after his victory at Hastings in 1066. Over the centuries it has served as a royal palace, a fortress and a prison, and it still houses the Crown Jewels. The Tower is guarded by the Yeoman Warders, popularly known as Beefeaters, and in 1988 it was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:db725b60aa0a','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When was the Tower of London added to the UNESCO World Heritage List?',array['in 1666','in 1988','in 2012','in 1066']::text[],'B','''In 1988 it was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.''',array['britain','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Standing on the north bank of the River Thames, the Tower of London was begun by William the Conqueror soon after his victory at Hastings in 1066. Over the centuries it has served as a royal palace, a fortress and a prison, and it still houses the Crown Jewels. The Tower is guarded by the Yeoman Warders, popularly known as Beefeaters, and in 1988 it was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:18cbcff34e28','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which role has the Tower NOT had, according to the passage?',array['a parliament building','a royal palace','a fortress','a prison']::text[],'A','The passage says the Tower has been a royal palace, a fortress and a prison; it does not mention a parliament.',array['britain','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Standing on the north bank of the River Thames, the Tower of London was begun by William the Conqueror soon after his victory at Hastings in 1066. Over the centuries it has served as a royal palace, a fortress and a prison, and it still houses the Crown Jewels. The Tower is guarded by the Yeoman Warders, popularly known as Beefeaters, and in 1988 it was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:f654ac40b026','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is Paul''s main concern about entry fees?',array['that parks are too crowded','that local residents pay too much','that conservation is unnecessary','that they could exclude poorer families']::text[],'D','''Fees risk excluding families on low incomes.''',array['dialogue','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Presenter: Should national parks charge entry fees?
Roza: I''m in favour. The money could fund conservation.
Paul: I see the logic, but fees risk excluding families on low incomes.
Roza: Then why not make entry free for local residents?
Paul: That might be a reasonable compromise.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6052dd349753','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How does the discussion about national parks end?',array['Both reject fees completely.','Roza changes her mind.','The presenter makes the decision.','Paul accepts a possible compromise.']::text[],'D','''That might be a reasonable compromise.''',array['dialogue']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Presenter: Should national parks charge entry fees?
Roza: I''m in favour. The money could fund conservation.
Paul: I see the logic, but fees risk excluding families on low incomes.
Roza: Then why not make entry free for local residents?
Paul: That might be a reasonable compromise.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:a5a8089fad1c','C1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','The Domesday Book, compiled in 1086 for William the Conqueror, was essentially a ___.',array['collection of poems','list of kings','survey of land and property','book of prayers']::text[],'C','It recorded who owned land and property in England.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:9b4b5f7a3849','C1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','In the Glorious Revolution of 1688, William of Orange replaced King ___ on the throne.',array['George III','James II','Henry VIII','Charles I']::text[],'B','James II fled and William and Mary took the throne.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:2fdf049820e6','C1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','Charles Darwin''s best-known theory is evolution by natural ___.',array['reflection','election','collection','selection']::text[],'D','Darwin''s ''On the Origin of Species'' (1859).',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:76fb4101c5fa','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In ___, King John was forced by his noblemen to agree to the Magna Carta.',array['1215','1314','1485','1066']::text[],'A','In 1215 King John agreed to the Magna Carta, which established that even the king was subject to the law.',array['britain','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:ea8ab38729d3','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which conclusion can be drawn from the timeline?',array['The Magna Carta was agreed in the 17th century.','The Great Fire happened before the Spanish Armada.','More than 600 years separate the Norman Conquest and the Act of Union.','The Act of Union and the Great Fire happened in the same century.']::text[],'C','1707 - 1066 = 641 years.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/uk_timeline.png','Five dates in British history','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:39288184642c','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Watch the chart. Between which two years shown did the UK population grow the most?',array['between 1901 and 1951','between 1851 and 1901','between 1951 and 2010','between 1801 and 1851']::text[],'B','The population rose from 20 million in 1851 to 40 million in 1901, an increase of 20 million: the largest rise shown.',array['britain','life_in_uk','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'video','samples/uk_population.mp4','UK population over time','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:703342ccce80','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which policy area remains reserved to Westminster?',array['defence','none of them','education','health']::text[],'A','''Defence and foreign affairs remain reserved.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Since 1997 the UK has seen a significant devolution of power. The Scottish Parliament and the National Assembly for Wales (renamed the Senedd in 2020) were established in 1999, while the Northern Ireland Assembly emerged from the 1998 Good Friday Agreement. The devolved administrations legislate on areas such as health and education, whereas defence and foreign affairs remain reserved to the UK Parliament at Westminster.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:b9f394a68f05','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the National Assembly for Wales renamed in 2020?',array['the Senedd','the Dail','Holyrood','Stormont']::text[],'A','''Renamed the Senedd in 2020''.',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Since 1997 the UK has seen a significant devolution of power. The Scottish Parliament and the National Assembly for Wales (renamed the Senedd in 2020) were established in 1999, while the Northern Ireland Assembly emerged from the 1998 Good Friday Agreement. The devolved administrations legislate on areas such as health and education, whereas defence and foreign affairs remain reserved to the UK Parliament at Westminster.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:fff1dfcab385','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What gave rise to the Northern Ireland Assembly?',array['the Good Friday Agreement','the Glorious Revolution','the Act of Union','the Magna Carta']::text[],'A','''Emerged from the 1998 Good Friday Agreement.''',array['life_in_uk','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Since 1997 the UK has seen a significant devolution of power. The Scottish Parliament and the National Assembly for Wales (renamed the Senedd in 2020) were established in 1999, while the Northern Ireland Assembly emerged from the 1998 Good Friday Agreement. The devolved administrations legislate on areas such as health and education, whereas defence and foreign affairs remain reserved to the UK Parliament at Westminster.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:63bd593a0cf7','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What point does the author make about ''first recorded'' uses?',array['They are always inaccurate.','They show that Shakespeare invented all of them.','They were added by later editors.','They do not prove that Shakespeare invented the expressions.']::text[],'D','''A first recorded use is not proof of invention.''',array['life_in_uk','culture','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Shakespeare''s influence on the English language is hard to overstate. Hundreds of words and phrases are first recorded in his plays and poems. Yet a first recorded use is not proof of invention: some expressions may simply reflect the everyday speech of his time, which happens to survive in his writing. Scholars therefore caution against crediting him with every phrase popularly attributed to him.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:1aee661faf44','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The author''s attitude to popular claims about Shakespeare''s inventions is best described as ___.',array['cautious','indifferent','hostile','enthusiastic']::text[],'A','Scholars ''caution against crediting him with every phrase''.',array['life_in_uk','culture','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Shakespeare''s influence on the English language is hard to overstate. Hundreds of words and phrases are first recorded in his plays and poems. Yet a first recorded use is not proof of invention: some expressions may simply reflect the everyday speech of his time, which happens to survive in his writing. Scholars therefore caution against crediting him with every phrase popularly attributed to him.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:bec864fe7aa1','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which language does the passage describe as Celtic?',array['Anglo-Saxon','Welsh','English','French']::text[],'B','''Welsh, a Celtic language, is spoken by many people in Wales.''',array['britain','culture','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','English is not the only language with deep roots in the UK. Welsh, a Celtic language, is spoken by many people in Wales and is taught in its schools; in parts of the Highlands and Islands of Scotland, Gaelic is still spoken, while some people in Northern Ireland speak Irish Gaelic. English itself was shaped by successive arrivals: the Anglo-Saxons brought the Germanic dialects at its core, and after the Norman Conquest of 1066 French added words such as ''park'' and ''beauty''.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:8145fabfc87a','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the passage, the Germanic core of English was brought by ___.',array['the Vikings','the Normans','the Romans','the Anglo-Saxons']::text[],'D','''The Anglo-Saxons brought the Germanic dialects at its core.''',array['britain','culture','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','English is not the only language with deep roots in the UK. Welsh, a Celtic language, is spoken by many people in Wales and is taught in its schools; in parts of the Highlands and Islands of Scotland, Gaelic is still spoken, while some people in Northern Ireland speak Irish Gaelic. English itself was shaped by successive arrivals: the Anglo-Saxons brought the Germanic dialects at its core, and after the Norman Conquest of 1066 French added words such as ''park'' and ''beauty''.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:24f0d6b900f5','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which words, according to the text, entered English from French after 1066?',array['''summer'' and ''winter''','''house'' and ''bread''','''park'' and ''beauty''','''apple'' and ''cow''']::text[],'C','''After the Norman Conquest of 1066 French added words such as park and beauty.''',array['britain','culture','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','English is not the only language with deep roots in the UK. Welsh, a Celtic language, is spoken by many people in Wales and is taught in its schools; in parts of the Highlands and Islands of Scotland, Gaelic is still spoken, while some people in Northern Ireland speak Irish Gaelic. English itself was shaped by successive arrivals: the Anglo-Saxons brought the Germanic dialects at its core, and after the Norman Conquest of 1066 French added words such as ''park'' and ''beauty''.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:1cabea5e0466','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What happens when peatland is drained?',array['It absorbs more carbon.','It releases the carbon it stores.','It turns into forest.','It stops decomposing.']::text[],'B','''Peat decomposes and releases this carbon.''',array['world_knowledge','nature','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Peatlands cover only around 3% of the Earth''s land surface, yet they are estimated to store more carbon than all the world''s forests combined. When drained for agriculture, peat decomposes and releases this carbon, turning a vital sink into a significant source of emissions. Restoration by re-wetting drained bogs is therefore increasingly seen as a cost-effective climate measure.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:520212a418f0','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the passage on peatlands, a ''sink'' is ___.',array['a place where water drains away','something that absorbs and stores carbon','a type of farm','a source of emissions']::text[],'B','A carbon sink takes in and stores carbon.',array['world_knowledge','nature','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Peatlands cover only around 3% of the Earth''s land surface, yet they are estimated to store more carbon than all the world''s forests combined. When drained for agriculture, peat decomposes and releases this carbon, turning a vital sink into a significant source of emissions. Restoration by re-wetting drained bogs is therefore increasingly seen as a cost-effective climate measure.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:c46123cab822','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why is re-wetting bogs increasingly favoured?',array['It is required by law everywhere.','It is a cost-effective way to reduce emissions.','It creates new farmland.','It increases the area covered by forests.']::text[],'B','''Increasingly seen as a cost-effective climate measure.''',array['world_knowledge','nature','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Peatlands cover only around 3% of the Earth''s land surface, yet they are estimated to store more carbon than all the world''s forests combined. When drained for agriculture, peat decomposes and releases this carbon, turning a vital sink into a significant source of emissions. Restoration by re-wetting drained bogs is therefore increasingly seen as a cost-effective climate measure.',null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:dfe61add9fa8','C2'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','To ''cross the Rubicon'' means to ___.',array['pass a point of no return','change sides repeatedly','travel abroad','avoid a difficult decision']::text[],'A','Julius Caesar crossed the river Rubicon in 49 BC, a step he could not undo.',array['world_knowledge','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:75569d422de9','C2'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','The Magna Carta is often cited as an early step towards the ___ of law.',array['throne','reign','crown','rule']::text[],'D','The ''rule of law'': no one is above the law.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:9814722a7da5','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which statement is best supported by the chart?',array['Population growth was the same in every period shown.','The population fell between 1901 and 1951.','The population grew more between 1801 and 1901 than between 1901 and 2010.','The population doubled between 1951 and 2010.']::text[],'C','1801-1901: from 8 to 40 million (+32 million); 1901-2010: from 40 to about 62 million (+22 million).',array['britain','life_in_uk','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/uk_population.png','UK population over time','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:026f08bb6b58','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which pair of events on the timeline is separated by the shortest interval?',array['the Magna Carta and the Spanish Armada','the Spanish Armada and the Great Fire of London','the Great Fire of London and the Act of Union','the Norman Conquest and the Magna Carta']::text[],'C','1666 to 1707 is only 41 years.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'image','samples/uk_timeline.png','Five dates in British history','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:5eeb91fcf87c','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Watch the timeline. Which event marks the creation of the Kingdom of Great Britain?',array['the Magna Carta','the Norman Conquest','the Act of Union','the Great Fire of London']::text[],'C','The Act of Union of 1707 united England and Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'video','samples/uk_timeline.mp4','Five dates in British history (10 seconds)','practice'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:612e8c5d6070','C2'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Watch the video. Approximately how many times larger was the UK population in 1901 than in 1801?',array['about ten times','about three times','about five times','about twice']::text[],'C','The population grew from 8 million in 1801 to 40 million in 1901: five times larger.',array['britain','life_in_uk','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03',null,'video','samples/uk_population.mp4','UK population over time','practice')
